  "text": "I can understand why you would feel angry. It's like, you're being told who you can and can't be friends with, and that can be really frustrating.\n\nWhen I was in a similar situation, I remember feeling really upset and angry too. But then I realized that my mom was coming from a place of love and concern, even if her methods weren't the best.\n\nI started talking to her about how I felt, and I explained to her that I understood where she was coming from, but I also needed some space to make my own choices. It wasn't easy, but we eventually found a way to compromise.\n\nOne thing that helped was when I asked her if she had ever made any mistakes when she was my age. She said yes, and that made me feel a little better. It made me realize that she wasn't perfect, and that she was trying her best.\n\nI also started to think about what I wanted to say to her, rather than just reacting with anger. I wanted to tell her that I loved her, but that I also needed some freedom to make my own choices. It was hard to put into words, but it helped me to feel more in control.\n\nIt might be helpful to talk to your mom about how you're feeling. You could say something like, \"Mom, I know you're coming from a place of love, but I feel really angry when you tell me who I can and can't be friends with. Can we find a way to compromise? I want to be able to make my own choices, but I also want to make sure you're happy.\"\n\nIt's okay to feel angry, and it's okay to express that anger in a way that feels safe for you. But it might also be helpful to try to understand where your mom is coming from, and to see if you can find a way to compromise.\n\nDoes that make sense?",
